<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Blue Jacket</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1743–c. 1808</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Weyapiersenwah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Blue Jacket was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a> war leader in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> country. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-turtle">Little Turtle</a> of the Miami he led the Western Confederacy of Native nations against American expansion, defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah-harmar">Josiah Harmar</a> in 1790 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-st-clair">Arthur St. Clair</a> in 1791. After the defeat at Fallen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">Timbers</a> in 1794 he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-greenville">Treaty of Greenville</a> in 1795, ceding much of Ohio.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Blue Jacket's early life. He first appears in written records in 1773, already a grown man and a war chief, when a British missionary visiting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a> villages on the Scioto River noted the site of Blue Jacket's Town on Deer Creek, in present-day Ross County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>. He took part in Lord Dunmore's War and fought on the British side in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, always seeking to keep Shawnee land rights. The British defeat cost the Shawnee valuable help in defending the Ohio Country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As white settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> grew, Blue Jacket became a leading figure of the resistance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northwest-indian-war">Northwest Indian War</a>, in which a confederacy of many nations fought the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. He was present during the Harmar campaign of 1790. On 3 November 1791 the confederate army under Blue Jacket and the Miami chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-turtle">Little Turtle</a> defeated the expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-st-clair">Arthur St. Clair</a>, governor of the Northwest Territory, at the Battle of the Wabash, the most severe defeat Native Americans ever inflicted on the United States. Traditional accounts give Little Turtle most of the credit; the historian John Sugden attributes this mainly to Little Turtle's own later self-promotion.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> then raised a new professional army under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-wayne">Anthony Wayne</a>, which defeated Blue Jacket's forces at the Battle of Fallen Timbers, south of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, on 20 August 1794. His son-in-law, Jacques Lasselle Jr., served as interpreter at Greenville in 1795. In 1805 Blue Jacket also signed the Treaty of Fort Industry, giving up still more of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, and in his last years he saw the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1877 a story was published claiming that Blue Jacket had been a white man, Marmaduke Van Swearingen, captured and adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a> in the 1770s. Allan W. Eckert's novels spread it, and an outdoor drama based on it ran in Xenia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, from 1981 to 2007. Historians since Helen Hornbeck Tanner in 1978 have rejected it, as Blue Jacket was older and already a chief, and a DNA study published in 2006 found that the two families did not match.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Blue Jacket” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The confederacy was a loose alliance of autonomous nations rather than a government, meeting in council and relying on war leaders such as Little Turtle of the Miami and <b>Blue Jacket</b> of the Shawnee.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-confederacy">Western Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Standing Rock Indian Reservation, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47,-105&amp;year=1785" aria-label="North America on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2087 513 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Blue Jacket"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2537 783h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2537 783h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47,-105&amp;year=1785">What was here in 1785? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
